{
 "id": "2022-li-sacred",
 "name": "Sacred Landscape Preference: Buddhist Temples, Outsiders vs Insiders",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Li, Ge & Wang (2022), \"Similarities and Differences in the Outsiders and Insiders' Visual Preferences on Sacred Landscape\", Frontiers in Psychology 13: 743933. Participants give age, gender, whether they identify as Buddhist and how often they visit Buddhist temples, then see the paper's 48 photographs of Han Chinese Buddhist temples in Suzhou (12 squares, 12 architecture, 12 waterscapes, 12 plant scenes) in random order, each after a 2 s fixation cross for 10 s, and rate landscape preference from -3 (not at all) to +3 (very much) with an optional reason. Photos are the paper's Figure 2 (CC BY 4.0). Analysis: mean preference by landscape type and insiders (Buddhist, regular visitors) vs outsiders (not Buddhist, never visited); the paper found architecture > waterscapes > plants > squares in both groups and much higher scores among insiders. Differences from the original study: (1) no eye tracking, so only the preference part is replicated; (2) each photo is rated right after viewing instead of all ratings after the eye-tracking block; (3) the reason is optional; (4) the two group questions are our own wording; (5) photos are cropped from the figure, about 255 x 150 px.",
